 wrote in <pan.2005.11.20.20.51.08.504098@decadence.it>:

> oh! you're working on IDE disks...
> the CMD646 IDE controller is bugged and requires a special workaround in
> the kernel to be turned on. I'm quite sure the boot kernel already has
> this option turned on, but common sense tells not to use IDE disks on PWS
> if you want some good performance.
> All I've ever done is putting a cdrom on IDE channel.

Here's an update:

	I had a 4.3 GB SCSII-2 disk that I'd acquired, thinking to try to
install it next to my existing 30 MB Atari MegaST4 disk...  I dehoused it
from the hot-swap caddy it was in and connected to the PWS's internal
SCSI cable.  Unfortunately, the disk housing in the box is for half-height
drives and this is full height, so it's still sitting on a cardboard box
next to the chassis...

	I fired up the Debian CD and decided to tell the installer to 
partition the disk automagically.  It did, and everything worked!

	When it came to do the post-install, I found the interface non-
intuitive.  Seems I eventually clicked on "install from net" rather than
"install from CD", so it madly exercised my ADSL connexion for about
1-1/2 hours downloading packages, then spent a similar time installing
them.  After it had finished I couldn't get X to start properly, so I
went to bed.

	The next day I realised that I didn't need to run X yet, as I
could ssh in from my laptop in the same way that I work on the computers
at Brunel and CERN.

	So, I fiddled around for the next few nights downloading BOINC
and SETI@HOME and apt-get-ting the extra packages they needed.  I
eventually got seti running but it was very slow, and if the state.sah
was to be believed, it was cranking at 26 Mflops!  After a couple of days
I found that I'd downloaded the new extended seti which does a much finer
analysis and takes around 10 times longer...

	I've now got the standard seti running, but it still looks like
it will take ~60 hours to crunch the test work-unit.  I have some
questions about optimising, but I'll start a new thread for that.

	Once I acquire another SCSI disk, I'll go back and try CentOS again.

-- 
