	Now that I have Debian Sarge running on my PWS500a, I've been porting
seti@home to the machine.  The performance has been disappointing; the
standard test work-unit is going to take around 60 hours to complete. boinc
benchmarks report:

2005-11-27 11:00:51 [---] Running CPU benchmarks
2005-11-27 11:01:50 [---] Benchmark results:
2005-11-27 11:01:50 [---]    Number of CPUs: 1
2005-11-27 11:01:50 [---]    153 double precision MIPS (Whetstone) per CPU
2005-11-27 11:01:50 [---]    555 integer MIPS (Dhrystone) per CPU
2005-11-27 11:01:50 [---] Finished CPU benchmarks

	I got gprof running and noticed a few things like:
o much of the time is spent in calculating log-gamma functions.  These
do a lot of casting to and from single-precision parameters/results.
o the total time that gprof reports is only about 1/3rd the cpu time charged
to the programme by top (e.g. a 39618-second run only totalled up 11376 seconds
of profile time).  Since this is unlikely to be profiler overhead, I'm
wondering if it's due to unaligned access or double/float conversion causing
execution problems.

	Can anyone suggest which g++ options [g++ (GCC) 3.3.5 
(Debian 1:3.3.5-13)] might improve the performance?  I've been looking for
flags to control alignment of floats, or promote all floats to doubles,
but I haven't found anything yet.

	Please cross-post this if you know of a better group to ask in.

