You may notice, I now have the symbolic links pointing to the updated
version of GLIBC and the compiler I wish to use GCC-3.2.3. You can get it
to work with later versions of GCC but you will have to mod the 
"create-comp-config.sh" file so that it won't generate too many warnings
and the like. I've managed to get it to run with GCC-4.0.1 but I 'rolled'
back to GCC-3.2.3 for various reasons...

After setting up your symbolic links then run the following command:

./create-comp-config.sh ccc-6.5.9.001-6 \
	/usr/lib/gcc-lib/alphapca56-alpha-linux-gnu/3.2.3

This will configure CCC/CXX to work with GCC-3.2.3 [change to the
version of GCC you're wanting to use... Also 'adjust' a couple of your
symbolic links].

Once you're finished with that, you can test it out on various programs.
I would advise using the CFLAG -std and CXXFLAG -std ansi so that Compaq
C won't be so restrictive.

Now, if you receive a lot of errors concerning GLIBC headers, parsing
errors mainly, you may need the GLIBC/Compaq C patch. I have one for
GLIBC-2.3.2 that works pretty good. I'm able to compile a large chunk of
my system with Compaq C...

I hope this has helped you out... Will L G

